virglrenderer: at critical memory pressure an app may replace its buffers and grow by a grace

What: virgl-darwin-memory-pressure.patch made every new big resource (16 MB or more, not
a screen or cursor) "for the desktop only" while macOS's memory pressure is critical, and
virgl-gpu-guard-desktop-reserve.patch loses the app context that takes one. So for as long as
macOS stayed critical, every big buffer an app made cost it its GPU context, also one that
only replaced a buffer it had just freed. Now, at critical, a new big resource has room when
- it fits in what macOS has left (free, inactive and purgeable memory: warn's rule), and
- all guest GPU memory (GL, Venus and the desktop's own: whose a new resource is is not known
  yet) stays within what was in use when critical began plus a grace: a 32nd of the Mac's
  memory, 256 MB to 1 GB (8 GB Mac: 256 MB, 16 GB: 512 MB, 32 GB and more: 1 GB).
So buffers that replace freed ones never cost a context, and steady growth at critical still
stops after the grace (that app is lost, as before). The baseline is set at the first look
that sees critical (a room check or the renderer's once-a-second look, whichever comes first)
and cleared when the renderer's own look sees less than critical; one log line says the
baseline and the grace. Warn, the desktop's own resources, screens and cursors, the budget and
its desktop reserve are unchanged. The rule is a pure function in src/virgl_gpu_guard.h
(virgl_gpu_guard_critical_room, virgl_gpu_guard_critical_grace); the baseline is renderer-thread
state beside the level. The test hook's level file takes LEVEL[:AVAILABLE_MB] like
OMACVM_GPU_MEMORY_PRESSURE, so tests do not depend on the build Mac's free memory.
Why: on a Mac mini with 16 GB (2026-10-30), a 12 GB VM with Chrome full screen at 5K: macOS
went critical (mostly the VM's memory kept it there for minutes: its graphics were about 5 GB
of the 14.5-17.7 GB it really held), Chrome's next window buffer (24-56 MB) cost Chrome its
context, and a Chrome started again was lost at its first window as long as critical lasted:
"chrome crashes and flickers unusable". A lost
context frees little (the app keeps its resources until its process ends), so the refusal
barely helped the Mac either.
Threading: the renderer thread only (QEMU's main loop), like the level it follows.
Upstream: not sent (macOS only; depends on the pressure and guard patches).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-gpu-guard-policy.sh (CI and the runtime build: the grace per Mac
size, replacement at the baseline, growth up to and past the grace, bigger than what macOS has
left, overflow); Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c (build time, Apple's software renderer):
mode critical-steady (critical, 4 GB left: chromium replaces a 64 MB buffer 20 times and is
never lost; it then grows by exactly the grace for this Mac, and the next one loses it;
Hyprland keeps its own), mode critical (40 MB left: a 32 MB texture is chromium's, a 64 MB one
for the desktop only), mode levelfile (critical:40 and back; the grace counts again from a new
critical period).
